    I added a small frontend fix so the login button won’t spin forever; it now times out after ~15s and shows an error if the backend isn’t reachable. I also wired the simulator into Docker and made web wait for a healthy backend.

    Start everything
    - From the repo root: `docker compose down -v && docker compose up -d --build`
    - Verify all services: `docker compose ps` (db healthy, backend healthy, web running, simulator running)

    Sign in
    - Visit http://localhost:8080
    - Enter username `owner` and password `owner`, click Sign in
    - If you still see a spinner or a timeout message:
      - Check containers: `docker compose ps`
      - Tail logs: `docker compose logs -f web backend`
      - Hard-refresh the page or try a private/incognito window

    Add a simulator-backed device
    - Go to Devices → New Device
    - Fill:
      - Name: any (e.g., TempSensor-1)
      - Unit: units (or leave blank)
      - Sampling Interval (s): 5
      - Thresholds (JSON): {"warning": 50, "critical": 80}
      - Modbus Config (JSON):
        {"type":"tcp","host":"simulator","port":1502,"unit_id":1,"register":0,"data_type":"uint16","byteorder":"big"}
    - Create
    - In the table, Status shows offline initially; within ~5–10 seconds it should switch to online
    - Click Test on the row; you should see a success toast

    Simulate outage and recovery
    - Stop simulator: `docker compose stop simulator`
    - After one polling interval, the device Status should flip to offline
    - Start simulator: `docker compose start simulator`
    - Status should return to online within one interval

    Verify scheduler persists across restart
    - Restart backend: `docker compose restart backend`
    - Wait one interval; the device Status remains online and continues updating

    If you want, I can add a small live value chip next to each device so you can watch the counter increment directly on the Devices page.
